// ============================================================
// ANECDOTES — the evidence layer for the Rubenzer facets
// ============================================================
// A facet score is an assertion. An anecdote is the reason.
// Each entry ties one concrete, sourced story to one leader and
// one facet, at the high or the low end of the scale.
//
//   l      leader id
//   f      facet key (ach com act int nvul ass nstr pos ten)
//   end    "high" | "low" — which end of the facet it illustrates
//   text   the anecdote
//   src    attribution where a specific source carries it
//   legend true when the story is famous and probably or certainly
//          apocryphal. These are kept rather than dropped, because
//          what a culture invents about a leader is evidence of a
//          kind — but they are marked so they are never mistaken
//          for record.
// ============================================================

window.ANECDOTES = [

  // ---------------------------------------------------------- ACTIVITY
  { l: "napoleon", f: "act", end: "high", text: "Ate at a sprint — meals put away in ten or fifteen minutes, which wrecked his digestion and was widely remarked on at court. He slept in broken intervals and rose repeatedly in the night to dictate, keeping secretaries on rotation so that no one had to sleep when he did not.", src: "Roberts, Napoleon: A Life" },
  { l: "napoleon", f: "act", end: "high", text: "On his own capacity for switching tasks: 'Different subjects are arranged in my head as in a chest of drawers. When I want to interrupt one piece of business, I close its drawer and open another.'" },
  { l: "clinton", f: "act", end: "high", text: "A famous short sleeper who ran the presidency on four or five hours and made policy calls at two in the morning, testing how little rest he could function on. He later concluded it had cost him: 'Every important mistake I've made in my life, I've made because I was too tired.'" },
  { l: "troosevelt", f: "act", end: "high", text: "Read a book most days, published some thirty-five of his own, boxed in the White House until a blow detached his left retina and blinded the eye, and then switched to jiu-jitsu rather than stop.", src: "Morris, The Rise of Theodore Roosevelt" },
  { l: "lbj", f: "act", end: "high", text: "Ran two working days inside one: up early, through to the late afternoon, then a change of pyjamas and a nap, then a second full day of meetings and calls that ran past midnight.", src: "Caro, The Years of Lyndon Johnson" },
  { l: "jcaesar", f: "act", end: "high", text: "Dictated to several secretaries simultaneously while travelling, and composed a technical treatise on Latin grammar, De Analogia, during a crossing of the Alps.", src: "Plutarch; Suetonius" },
  { l: "peter1", f: "act", end: "high", text: "Learned fourteen trades, worked incognito as a shipwright in the yards of Zaandam and Deptford, and practised dentistry on his courtiers — he kept the extracted teeth in a bag as a record." },
  { l: "qinshihuang", f: "act", end: "high", text: "Set himself a daily quota of state documents measured by weight in bamboo slips, and would not sleep until he had worked through it.", src: "Sima Qian, Shiji" },
  { l: "thatcher", f: "act", end: "high", text: "Ran on four hours' sleep and routinely knew a department's brief better than its own minister. The night the Brighton bomb destroyed her hotel bathroom she finished her conference speech and delivered it that morning, on time." },
  { l: "churchill", f: "act", end: "high", text: "Dictated from the bath and from bed, worked past two in the morning, and protected an afternoon nap specifically so that he could extract two working days from one — a habit he defended as a wartime necessity." },
  { l: "justinian", f: "act", end: "high", text: "Known to contemporaries as 'the emperor who never sleeps'; Procopius describes him walking the palace corridors at night, and in one reign he recodified all Roman law, rebuilt Hagia Sophia and attempted the reconquest of the West." },
  { l: "modi", f: "act", end: "high", text: "Reported to work eighteen-hour days and to take essentially no holidays across more than a decade in office — a pace not disputed even by his critics." },
  { l: "hueylong", f: "act", end: "high", text: "Governed Louisiana from a hotel suite, dictating bills to stenographers and sending them to a legislature he had already counted, sometimes passing dozens in a single sitting." },
  { l: "coolidge", f: "act", end: "low", text: "Slept around eleven hours in every twenty-four, including a long afternoon nap, and regarded the restraint as a governing philosophy rather than an indulgence: most problems, he thought, would resolve themselves if a president declined to meddle." },
  { l: "reagan", f: "act", end: "low", text: "On his own working habits: 'It's true hard work never killed anybody, but I figure, why take the chance?' He kept short days, napped, and delegated more radically than any modern president." },
  { l: "taft", f: "act", end: "low", text: "Fell asleep in cabinet meetings, in conversation, at the theatre and reportedly at a funeral — near-certainly untreated sleep apnoea at well over three hundred pounds, and the lowest Activity reading in the presidential set after Coolidge." },
  { l: "harding", f: "act", end: "low", text: "Structured the week around two rounds of golf and two evenings of poker, and made little attempt to disguise that the job's ceremonial half suited him better than its working half." },
  { l: "peron", f: "act", end: "high", text: "His own account of the Labour Secretariat in 1943–45, given to the cadres of the Peronist school in 1951: he received everyone who came — 'if ten came, I spoke to them; if two came, I spoke to them too; if there were ten thousand, so much the better' — preferred to talk to organisations rather than to individuals, because to gather ants you go to the anthill, and claimed to have worked from five in the morning until five the next morning. The hours are his boast; the stream of delegations through the Secretariat is confirmed by others.", src: "Perón, Conducción política (lecture of 29 March 1951); James, Resistance and Integration (1988)" },
  { l: "palmerston", f: "act", end: "high", text: "In July 1864, aged 79, he survived a vote of censure over Denmark by eighteen votes. The result was announced at half past two in the morning, and he ran up the stairs to the Ladies' Gallery to embrace his wife. Disraeli: 'What pluck to mount those dreadful stairs at three o'clock in the morning, and eighty years of age!'", src: "Disraeli, as quoted by Palmerston's biographers" },
  { l: "gladstone", f: "act", end: "high", text: "Took up felling trees at Hawarden in 1858 and kept at it until he was 81, as the 'rational recreation' that refreshed him for work. Lord Randolph Churchill: 'The forest laments in order that Mr Gladstone may perspire.' Less noticed, he planted saplings to replace what he cut." },
  { l: "lloydgeorge", f: "act", end: "high", text: "Made Minister of Munitions in May 1915, he brought in businessmen to run a new department with its own national factories, and within a year the ministry was the largest buyer, seller and employer in the country. Historians still argue how much of the output gain was his and how much was already under way.", src: "Wikipedia, David Lloyd George (munitions section); Grigg, Lloyd George: From Peace to War (1985)" },
  { l: "asquith", f: "act", end: "low", text: "His habits were the opposite of his successor's: bridge after dinner, a full social round, weekend motor drives, and hours spent writing to Venetia Stanley — sometimes during cabinet. Lord Crawford found him 'somnolent' in cabinet, and Lloyd George's friend Lord Riddell recorded that he never moved until he was forced. 'Wait and see', a phrase from 1910, was turned on him in the war.", src: "Riddell diaries; Crawford diaries; Brock & Brock, H. H. Asquith: Letters to Venetia Stanley (1982)" },
  { l: "baldwin", f: "act", end: "low", text: "Took long summer holidays at Aix-les-Bains with his wife. In 1928, as prime minister, he left London early in August and did not return until late September, leaving the Lord Chancellor, Lord Hailsham, to act for him. Colleagues and critics alike thought him idle — a reputation he did little to dispel.", src: "Contemporary press reports (1928, 1935); Middlemas & Barnes, Baldwin (1969)" },
  { l: "blair", f: "act", end: "high", text: "Flew to Belfast on 7 April 1998 with the talks close to collapse and stayed until they finished: three days of negotiation at Hillsborough Castle and Stormont, much of it through the night with David Trimble, John Hume, Gerry Adams and the Irish taoiseach Bertie Ahern, until the Good Friday Agreement was concluded on the afternoon of 10 April.", src: "Jonathan Powell, Great Hatred, Little Room (2008); Blair, A Journey" },
  { l: "grey", f: "act", end: "low", text: "Leader of the Whigs for most of twenty-four years in opposition, he was notoriously reluctant to make the journey from Howick in Northumberland to London, did not keep up an active leadership, and more than once offered to retire — the leader who came to power at sixty-six had spent much of his career trying not to be in Westminster.", src: "Wikipedia, citing Smith, Lord Grey" },

  // ---------------------------------------------------------- ACHIEVEMENT STRIVING
  { l: "jcaesar", f: "ach", end: "high", text: "In Spain at thirty-odd, reading a history of Alexander, he fell silent and then wept — Alexander had conquered the world at an age by which he had done nothing memorable.", src: "Plutarch; Suetonius" },
  { l: "jcaesar", f: "ach", end: "high", text: "Passing a wretched Alpine village, his companions joked about whether even there men competed for office. He answered that he would rather be first among these people than second in Rome.", src: "Plutarch" },
  { l: "lbj", f: "ach", end: "high", text: "As a twenty-three-year-old congressional aide sharing a basement dormitory, he took four showers an evening and brushed his teeth five times — each trip a fresh chance to meet the other aides in the communal bathroom and build a network.", src: "Caro, The Path to Power" },
  { l: "napoleon", f: "ach", end: "high", text: "'Impossible is a word to be found only in the dictionary of fools' — and, more revealingly, his remark that he had never been master of his own plans because circumstances always ran ahead of him." },
  { l: "alexander", f: "ach", end: "high", text: "Plutarch has him weeping at the thought that there were no worlds left to conquer. The story is almost certainly a later moralising invention, and it survived because it fitted what everyone already believed about him.", legend: true },
  { l: "thatcher", f: "ach", end: "high", text: "As Education Secretary she read every brief in the red box; as prime minister she arrived at meetings having mastered the material of the minister whose department it was, which was how arguments were won." },
  { l: "hoover", f: "ach", end: "high", text: "Made a fortune as a mining engineer before forty, then refused a salary for every public position he ever held, including the presidency, donating it instead." },
  { l: "harding", f: "ach", end: "low", text: "Of the office he had won: 'I am not fit for this office and should never have been here.' Few leaders in this index have assessed themselves so accurately." },
  { l: "coolidge", f: "ach", end: "low", text: "Ended his own career in nine words handed to reporters on slips of paper: 'I do not choose to run for President in 1928.' He offered no elaboration then or later." },
  { l: "taft", f: "ach", end: "low", text: "He had wanted the Supreme Court his whole life and endured the presidency as a detour. Appointed Chief Justice in 1921, he remarked that he could not remember that he had ever been president." },

  { l: "parkchunghee", f: "ach", end: "high", text: "Launched the Heavy and Chemical Industry drive in 1973 over strong objections from the World Bank, building on the state steel company POSCO, created in 1968 with Japanese financial and technical help, in a country whose exports were still mostly light manufactures." },
  { l: "pittyounger", f: "ach", end: "high", text: "Sent up to Cambridge at fourteen, in Parliament at twenty-one, Chancellor of the Exchequer at twenty-three, he turned down the King's first offer of the premiership in early 1783 because he could not yet command the Commons — and took it that December, at twenty-four, when he judged he could hold it. The wits called it a 'mince-pie administration' that would not outlast Christmas; it lasted seventeen years.", src: "Ehrman, The Younger Pitt; Hague, William Pitt the Younger" },
  { l: "heath", f: "ach", end: "high", text: "Took up ocean racing in his fifties: he bought the first Morning Cloud in 1969 and won that year's Sydney to Hobart race, and in 1971, while prime minister, captained the British team that won the Admiral's Cup, the unofficial world championship of offshore racing.", src: "Heath, Sailing: A Course of My Life (1975); Ziegler, Edward Heath" },

  // ---------------------------------------------------------- COMPETENCE
  { l: "jcaesar", f: "com", end: "high", text: "Captured by Cilician pirates as a young man, he was told his ransom was twenty talents and objected that they had no idea who they had taken — he told them to demand fifty. He joked throughout his captivity that he would return and crucify them, and on release he raised a fleet and did.", src: "Plutarch" },
  { l: "degaulle", f: "com", end: "high", text: "In June 1940, holding no office, commanding nobody and legally a fugitive, he broadcast from London on the assumption that he personally constituted the legitimate French state. The self-belief preceded any evidence for it by several years." },
  { l: "eisenhower", f: "com", end: "high", text: "Before D-Day he wrote and pocketed a note accepting sole blame should the landings fail: 'If any blame or fault attaches to the attempt it is mine alone.' He misdated it July instead of June, which is the only sign of strain in the document." },
  { l: "leekuanyew", f: "com", end: "high", text: "Asked whether he had ever doubted the methods, he replied that he had not, and that the results were the argument. The certainty was not a pose and is most of why the model reads him where it does." },
  { l: "charles5", f: "com", end: "low", text: "Ruler of an empire on which the sun never set, he abdicated in 1556 from exhaustion and self-doubt — the only major monarch in this index to quit on the grounds that he could no longer decide fast enough — and retired to a monastery at Yuste." },
  { l: "harding", f: "com", end: "low", text: "To a friend: 'I am a man of limited talents from a small town. I don't seem to grasp that I am president.'" },
  { l: "salazar", f: "com", end: "high", text: "Called back to the Finance Ministry in April 1928 by a military dictatorship near bankruptcy, he accepted only on condition that he could veto the spending of every other ministry. Within a year the Portuguese budget showed its first surplus in years, and the professor whom the officers had hired as a technician became the man they could not do without; four years later he was prime minister.", src: "Meneses, Salazar: A Political Biography (2009)" },
  { l: "asquith", f: "com", end: "high", text: "In November 1910 he extracted from George V a secret promise to create enough Liberal peers to outvote the House of Lords, then fought the December election and kept the promise private until July 1911, when the threat alone forced the Parliament Bill through. Roy Jenkins called it a masterly display of political nerve and patient determination.", src: "Jenkins, Asquith (1964)" },
  { l: "liverpool", f: "com", end: "high", text: "Before he became prime minister at forty-two he had held every secretaryship of state — Foreign (negotiating the Peace of Amiens), Home and War. R. W. Seton-Watson thought this gave him an 'unrivalled insight into the whole machinery of government', and the reason he lasted more than twice as long as any other successor of Pitt.", src: "R. W. Seton-Watson, Britain in Europe 1789–1914 (1937)" },

  // ---------------------------------------------------------- INTELLECT
  { l: "jefferson", f: "int", end: "high", text: "Kennedy, hosting forty-nine Nobel laureates in 1962, called it 'the most extraordinary collection of talent that has ever been gathered at the White House, with the possible exception of when Thomas Jefferson dined alone.'" },
  { l: "jefferson", f: "int", end: "high", text: "Having sold his library to the nation after the British burned the Capitol's collection — it became the Library of Congress — he immediately began buying again: 'I cannot live without books.'" },
  { l: "akbar", f: "int", end: "high", text: "Almost certainly dyslexic and never able to read fluently, he kept a library of some twenty-four thousand volumes and had them read aloud to him, and convened the Ibadat Khana debates where Muslims, Hindus, Jains, Zoroastrians and Jesuits argued in his presence." },
  { l: "frederick2p", f: "int", end: "high", text: "Composed over a hundred sonatas for the flute, wrote history and philosophy in French, corresponded with Voltaire for forty years, and published the Anti-Machiavel — a refutation of exactly the conduct he would spend his reign practising." },
  { l: "marcusaurelius", f: "int", end: "high", text: "Wrote the Meditations in Greek, for himself alone, in camp on the Danube frontier during a plague and a war. It is the only sustained private philosophical self-examination by a ruling head of state that survives from the ancient world." },
  { l: "catherine2", f: "int", end: "high", text: "Bought Diderot's library when he needed money, left the books in his house, and paid him a salary as its librarian — the most elegant subsidy in the history of patronage." },
  { l: "kangxi", f: "int", end: "high", text: "Studied Euclid and algebra with the Jesuits, practised anatomical dissection, and recorded in his own words the satisfaction of getting a calculation right — unusual candour about intellectual pleasure from a Chinese emperor.", src: "Spence, Emperor of China" },
  { l: "elizabeth1", f: "int", end: "high", text: "Translated Boethius for her own amusement, and when a Polish ambassador delivered an insulting address in Latin, answered him extempore in Latin so sharply that Robert Cecil reported she had been 'in a great chafe' and had done superbly." },
  { l: "suleiman", f: "int", end: "high", text: "Wrote over two thousand poems under the pen name Muhibbi — 'the lover' — including verse to Hürrem that circulated in the palace. The literary seriousness was not courtly convention." },
  { l: "cleopatra", f: "int", end: "high", text: "Plutarch records that she spoke with Ethiopians, Hebrews, Arabs, Syrians, Medes and Parthians in their own languages, and that she was the first of her Greek-speaking dynasty in three centuries to bother learning Egyptian." },
  { l: "churchill", f: "int", end: "high", text: "Won the Nobel Prize in Literature in 1953 — not for a novel but for the histories and the speeches — and is the only leader in this index whose principal weapon was his own prose." },
  { l: "washington", f: "int", end: "low", text: "His own colleagues thought his mind unremarkable and his judgement exceptional. Jefferson wrote that his mind was 'slow in operation' and that his conclusions, once reached, were almost never wrong — a distinction this facet handles badly." },
  { l: "jackson", f: "int", end: "low", text: "'It's a damn poor mind that can only think of one way to spell a word' is the line everyone quotes; there is no contemporary source for it, and it stuck because it matched the man.", legend: true },
  { l: "walpole", f: "int", end: "low", text: "Lord Hardwicke recalled that Walpole would put aside a pile of official letters unopened, but if he saw his huntsman's hand on one he would read it at once. In retirement, when his son offered to read history to him, he is said to have answered: 'Anything but history, for history must be false.' (The popular versions make the huntsman a gamekeeper.)", src: "Walpoliana (1783, 1799), as collected on Wikiquote; Coxe, Memoirs of Sir Robert Walpole (1798)" },
  { l: "gladstone", f: "int", end: "high", text: "His diary, kept from 1825 to 1896, records some 20,500 books read. At 85 he gave £40,000 and most of his 32,000 volumes to found St Deiniol's Library at Hawarden, helping to wheel the books three-quarters of a mile from the castle and shelving them himself in his own classification.", src: "Foot & Matthew (eds.), The Gladstone Diaries, index volume; Gladstone's Library" },
  { l: "salisbury", f: "int", end: "high", text: "Kept a laboratory at Hatfield where he experimented with electricity; he installed electric bells early, and for a time arc lighting in the dining room, to the distress of his family and guests. A Fellow of the Royal Society from 1869, he was president of the British Association in 1894.", src: "gov.uk, Past Prime Ministers; Electrifying the Country House project (Hatfield)" },
  { l: "asquith", f: "int", end: "high", text: "A slow start at the bar ended in 1889 when, as junior counsel before the Parnell Commission, his cross-examination of The Times's manager exposed how little the paper knew about the provenance of the letters it had printed as Parnell's — letters that were soon shown to be forgeries. He took silk within a year and was Home Secretary three years later.", src: "Jenkins, Asquith (1964)" },
  { l: "macmillan", f: "int", end: "high", text: "Minister Resident at Allied headquarters in Algiers from 1942, he explained the British position to Richard Crossman as that of cultivated Greeks advising the Romans: 'We, my dear Crossman, are the Greeks in the American empire.' The line became his working theory of the special relationship.", src: "Crossman's recollection, quoted in Horne, Macmillan" },
  { l: "haroldwilson", f: "int", end: "high", text: "Took a first in philosophy, politics and economics at Oxford, worked for William Beveridge on unemployment, ran the statistics of the wartime coal industry, and in September 1947, at thirty-one, entered Attlee's cabinet as President of the Board of Trade — the youngest cabinet minister of the century.", src: "Pimlott, Harold Wilson; Ziegler, Wilson" },

  // ---------------------------------------------------------- NOT VULNERABLE
  { l: "troosevelt", f: "nvul", end: "high", text: "Shot in the chest in Milwaukee in 1912, with the bullet slowed by his folded fifty-page speech and a steel spectacle case, he declined the hospital and spoke for ninety minutes: 'It takes more than that to kill a Bull Moose.'" },
  { l: "tito", f: "nvul", end: "high", text: "After surviving repeated Soviet assassination attempts he wrote to Stalin: 'Stop sending people to kill me. If you don't stop, I will send one man to Moscow and I won't have to send another.' The letter was found in Stalin's desk after his death.", legend: true },
  { l: "washington", f: "nvul", end: "high", text: "Facing a mutinous officer corps at Newburgh in 1783, he drew out a letter and then fumbled for the spectacles almost none of them had seen him wear: 'Gentlemen, you must pardon me. I have grown grey in your service and now find myself growing blind.' The mutiny dissolved in tears." },
  { l: "zelensky", f: "nvul", end: "high", text: "Offered evacuation from Kyiv with a Russian column approaching, his reported answer — 'I need ammunition, not a ride' — became the war's defining line and, in facet terms, as clean a test of composure as the record offers." },
  { l: "juarez", f: "nvul", end: "high", text: "For years of French occupation the Mexican republic was wherever his black carriage happened to be. He simply declined to stop being the government, and outlasted an emperor installed by the greatest army in Europe." },
  { l: "deng", f: "nvul", end: "high", text: "Purged twice, sent to labour in a tractor factory, and with a son crippled by Red Guards, he returned to power at seventy-three without evident rancour and reorganised a fifth of humanity's economy." },
  { l: "ieyasu", f: "nvul", end: "high", text: "Spent much of his childhood as a hostage of rival clans and, on being told an enemy demanded the death of his own son and wife as proof of loyalty, complied. He then waited another four decades for the shogunate." },
  { l: "napoleon", f: "nvul", end: "low", text: "At Fontainebleau in April 1814, with the marshals refusing to march, he swallowed the opium and belladonna he had carried since the Russian retreat. The dose had degraded and he survived it, and never referred to the attempt again.", src: "Caulaincourt" },
  { l: "justinian", f: "nvul", end: "low", text: "During the Nika riots he had packed the treasure and was boarding ships to flee when Theodora refused to move — 'the purple makes a fine shroud' — and shamed the court into staying. The counter-attack killed tens of thousands in the Hippodrome.", src: "Procopius" },
  { l: "frederick2p", f: "nvul", end: "low", text: "Through the worst of the Seven Years' War he carried a phial of poison in a small box and wrote letters that read as suicide notes, telling his sister that he would not survive the ruin of the state." },
  { l: "alexander", f: "nvul", end: "low", text: "Having run Cleitus through with a spear during a drunken quarrel — Cleitus had saved his life at the Granicus — he tried to kill himself with the same weapon, then lay three days refusing food or water.", src: "Arrian; Plutarch" },
  { l: "nixon", f: "nvul", end: "low", text: "In the final months aides described him drinking heavily, ordering fires lit in the White House fireplaces in summer with the air conditioning running, and, by Woodward and Bernstein's account, talking to the portraits of his predecessors.", src: "Woodward & Bernstein, The Final Days" },
  { l: "stalin", f: "nvul", end: "low", text: "After the German invasion he withdrew to his dacha for the best part of a fortnight and did not broadcast. When the Politburo arrived he reportedly assumed they had come to arrest him; they had come to ask him to lead." },
  { l: "nasser", f: "nvul", end: "low", text: "After the 1967 defeat he resigned live on television, visibly broken. Crowds filled the streets demanding he stay, and he did — and was dead three years later at fifty-two." },
  { l: "victoria", f: "nvul", end: "low", text: "After Albert's death in 1861 she withdrew almost entirely for a decade, had his clothes laid out daily, and damaged the monarchy's standing badly enough that republican sentiment became a live political force." },

  { l: "suharto", f: "nvul", end: "high", text: "On the night of 30 September 1965 he was at the army hospital beside his three-year-old son Tommy, who had been scalded, and the conspirators' colonel visited him there. By dawn on 1 October he was at Kostrad headquarters, and within a day he had mobilised Kostrad and the para-commandos and retaken the radio station and central Jakarta without a fight. Colonel Latief later testified that he had told Suharto of the plan; Suharto's own accounts differ, and the exchange remains disputed." },
  { l: "parkchunghee", f: "nvul", end: "high", text: "On 15 August 1974 a gunman fired at him from the front row during a speech at the National Theater; a stray bullet struck his wife, Yuk Young-soo, who died that day. He continued his speech as she was carried from the stage." },
  { l: "mussolini", f: "nvul", end: "low", text: "In July 1943, ill and with Sicily invaded, he let the Grand Council of Fascism meet on 24 July, sat through a session that ran past two in the morning, and allowed the vote on Dino Grandi's motion to remove his military powers — which passed 19 to 8. The next afternoon he went to his regular audience with the King and was arrested by carabinieri as he left.", src: "Bosworth, Mussolini" },
  { l: "berlusconi", f: "nvul", end: "high", text: "On 13 December 2009, after a rally in Milan's Piazza del Duomo, a man threw a small metal model of the cathedral into his face, breaking his nose and two teeth. He was photographed bloodied and kept in hospital for several days; the attacker, Massimo Tartaglia, had a history of mental illness.", src: "Contemporary press reports (AP, Reuters), December 2009" },
  { l: "franco", f: "nvul", end: "high", text: "In June 1916, a 23-year-old captain of Moroccan regulars, he was shot in the abdomen at El Biutz — a wound that usually killed in the field medicine of the day. He survived, and when the army refused him promotion he appealed over its head to King Alfonso XIII and got it, becoming in 1917 the youngest major in the army. Among his Moroccan troops he had a name for 'baraka', a charmed immunity under fire.", src: "Preston, Franco (1993)" },
  { l: "pinochet", f: "nvul", end: "high", text: "On 7 September 1986 guerrillas of the Manuel Rodríguez Patriotic Front ambushed his motorcade on the road back from his country house in the Cajón del Maipo with rifles and rockets; five of his escort were killed. He escaped with minor injuries — a rocket that hit his car failed to explode — and afterwards had posters printed of what he said was the image of the Virgin of Carmen in the shattered glass, captioned with thanks to her for saving Chile. Within hours, security agents had killed four opponents in reprisal.", src: "Contemporary press; El Desconcierto (2018); The Clinic (2016)" },
  { l: "peron", f: "nvul", end: "low", text: "Faced with the military and naval revolt of September 1955, he did not call out the unions, whose leaders had offered to fight. He sent the army a letter that its generals chose to read as a resignation, took refuge in the Paraguayan embassy and then on a Paraguayan gunboat in the port of Buenos Aires, and left for exile. Biographers still argue whether it was a loss of nerve or a refusal to start a civil war — two weeks earlier he had promised the crowd five of theirs for every one of his.", src: "Page, Perón: A Biography (1983)" },
  { l: "pittyounger", f: "nvul", end: "high", text: "On Whit Sunday, 27 May 1798, after the opposition MP George Tierney took offence at his words in the Commons, the prime minister fought a duel with him on Putney Heath. Both missed at twelve paces; on the second exchange Pitt fired into the air, and the seconds ended it.", src: "Wikipedia, citing Hague and Ehrman; contemporary accounts" },
  { l: "peel", f: "nvul", end: "low", text: "On 17 February 1843, four weeks after his private secretary Edward Drummond had been shot in mistake for him, Peel heard Richard Cobden say in the Commons that he held the prime minister 'individually responsible' for the distress of the country. Peel rose in visible agitation and treated the phrase as an incitement to assassination; Cobden's explanation was drowned out by the government benches, and the breach lasted until Peel, resigning in 1846, gave the credit for repeal to Cobden by name.", src: "Hansard, 17 February 1843; Gash, Sir Robert Peel; Hurd, Robert Peel" },
  { l: "salisbury", f: "nvul", end: "low", text: "Bullied so badly at Eton that he was taken away in 1845, he grew up convinced, his biographers argue, that most people were cowardly and cruel and that the mob would trample sensitive individuals — the root of his pessimism about democracy. As an adult he suffered recurrent bouts of depression.", src: "Roberts, Salisbury: Victorian Titan; Paul Smith, ODNB" },
  { l: "lloydgeorge", f: "nvul", end: "high", text: "In December 1901, at the height of his opposition to the Boer War, he insisted on speaking at Birmingham Town Hall, in Joseph Chamberlain's own city. A hostile crowd besieged the hall, and he had to be smuggled out disguised as a policeman.", src: "Grigg, The Young Lloyd George (1973); Wikipedia, David Lloyd George" },
  { l: "asquith", f: "nvul", end: "low", text: "On 12 May 1915, in the fortnight of the shell scandal and Admiral Fisher's resignation that ended his Liberal government, Venetia Stanley told him she would marry Edwin Montagu. He replied: 'As you know well, this breaks my heart.' Within two weeks he had formed a coalition. How much the blow affected his judgement is still argued.", src: "Brock & Brock, H. H. Asquith: Letters to Venetia Stanley (1982)" },
  { l: "attlee", f: "nvul", end: "high", text: "On 26 July 1945, with Labour's landslide declared, Herbert Morrison argued that the new parliamentary party should choose its leader before anyone went to the Palace. Attlee did not wait for the argument: that evening his wife Violet drove him to Buckingham Palace, he accepted the King's commission, and the question was closed.", src: "Harris, Attlee; Bew, Citizen Clem" },
  { l: "macmillan", f: "nvul", end: "high", text: "Severely wounded at Flers–Courcelette on the Somme in September 1916, he lay for more than twelve hours in a shell hole, sometimes feigning death as Germans passed, and read Aeschylus in Greek from the copy he carried.", src: "Horne, Macmillan; Thorpe, Supermac" },
  { l: "liverpool", f: "nvul", end: "low", text: "Outwardly calm, he was in his recent biographer's phrase 'a bag of nerves': he is said to have trembled before opening his letters, was given to tears, and was made physically ill by a quarrel with Wellington over a bishopric. Mrs Arbuthnot found him in one crisis 'frightened to death lest his colleagues should desert'.", src: "W. A. Hay, Lord Liverpool (2018), as reviewed by Lord Lexden; Mrs Arbuthnot's Journal (1821)" },
  { l: "grey", f: "nvul", end: "low", text: "After his first speech in the Lords in January 1808 he wrote to his wife that it was impossible he 'should ever do anything there worth thinking of'. Twenty-six years later, with his cabinet breaking up over Ireland, he told her he felt 'depressed and totally deprived of all energy and power, both physical and mental' — and resigned within weeks.", src: "Grey's letters to Lady Grey, quoted by Smith and on Wikipedia" },
  { l: "haroldwilson", f: "nvul", end: "low", text: "Weeks after leaving office in 1976 he invited two BBC journalists, Barrie Penrose and Roger Courtiour, to investigate his belief that elements in MI5 and South African intelligence had worked to smear him. Christopher Andrew's authorised history of MI5 (2009) concluded that there had been no plot by the service as such, while confirming that some officers held hostile suspicions of him; how far any of them went is still disputed, and the episode is usually read as evidence of the suspicion that had grown on him in office.", src: "Penrose and Courtiour, The Pencourt File (1978); Christopher Andrew, The Defence of the Realm (2009)" },

  // ---------------------------------------------------------- ASSERTIVENESS
  { l: "jackson", f: "ass", end: "high", text: "Fought a duel with Charles Dickinson in 1806 by letting Dickinson fire first, taking the bullet an inch from his heart, standing still, and then shooting him dead. He carried that bullet for the rest of his life." },
  { l: "lbj", f: "ass", end: "high", text: "'The Treatment' was physical: he would advance until his face was inches away, grip lapels or shoulders, and alternate flattery, pleading and threat for as long as it took. Photographs of him working Senator Theodore Green show it as a kind of assault." },
  { l: "peter1", f: "ass", end: "high", text: "At a reception for the returning boyars in 1698 he produced a razor and began shaving their beards himself, in front of the court, then taxed the ones who wanted to keep them." },
  { l: "bengurion", f: "ass", end: "high", text: "Declared independence in May 1948 over the objections of much of his own cabinet and against American advice, and within two months ordered the shelling of the Altalena — an arms ship belonging to his own side — to establish that the state would hold a monopoly on force." },
  { l: "hueylong", f: "ass", end: "high", text: "He did not persuade the Louisiana legislature so much as acquire it: he was impeached in 1929 and survived by producing a round robin of fifteen senators pledged in advance to acquit him regardless of the evidence." },
  { l: "rjdaley", f: "nvul", end: "low", text: "When Senator Abraham Ribicoff denounced 'Gestapo tactics on the streets of Chicago' from the podium of the 1968 Democratic convention, Daley rose in the front row and shouted back at him on live television. Lip-readers read an obscenity; Daley's people said he had called Ribicoff a faker.", src: "Cohen & Taylor, American Pharaoh" },
  { l: "rjdaley", f: "ten", end: "low", text: "After the riots that followed Martin Luther King's assassination in April 1968, he announced that he had ordered the police superintendent to 'shoot to kill any arsonist' and 'shoot to maim or cripple anyone looting' — and complained that the police had been too restrained.", src: "Royko, Boss; Cohen & Taylor, American Pharaoh" },
  { l: "rjdaley", f: "int", end: "low", text: "Defending his police after the convention, he told reporters that 'the policeman isn't there to create disorder, the policeman is there to preserve disorder.' His public speech was a running collection of such slips — which is why this facet's band is wide: the same man knew the city budget line by line, having learned it as state revenue director and county clerk.", src: "Royko, Boss" },
  { l: "jefferson", f: "ass", end: "low", text: "He delivered exactly two public speeches as president — his two inaugural addresses — and sent his annual messages to Congress in writing rather than appear in person, a precedent that held for over a century." },
  { l: "merkel", f: "ass", end: "low", text: "Her method produced a German verb. 'Merkeln' means to decline to decide, to wait, to let a position exhaust itself — coined as mockery and functionally an accurate description of a deliberate strategy that outlasted four French presidents." },
  { l: "coolidge", f: "ass", end: "low", text: "A dinner guest told him she had bet she could get more than two words out of him. 'You lose,' he is supposed to have replied. He denied the story, which did nothing to slow its circulation.", legend: true },
  { l: "mussolini", f: "ass", end: "high", text: "On 3 January 1925, with his government shaken by the murder of Giacomo Matteotti, he told the Chamber of Deputies that he alone assumed 'the political, moral and historical responsibility' for all that had happened, and that if Fascism had been a criminal association, he was its chief. The dictatorship was built over the two years that followed.", src: "Speech to the Chamber of Deputies, 3 January 1925" },
  { l: "berlusconi", f: "ass", end: "high", text: "At a press conference in Sofia on 18 April 2002, as prime minister, he accused the RAI journalists Enzo Biagi and Michele Santoro and the comedian Daniele Luttazzi of a 'criminal' use of public television and said the new RAI management had a duty to stop it. All three programmes were dropped within months, officially for commercial reasons; Biagi and Santoro returned to RAI only after his coalition lost in 2006.", src: "Il Post, 'Cosa fu l'editto bulgaro' (2022)" },
  { l: "franco", f: "ass", end: "high", text: "On 19 April 1937, without consulting the parties concerned, he decreed the fusion of the Falange and the Carlists into a single party with himself as its chief. When the Falange's acting leader, Manuel Hedilla, resisted, he was arrested and twice sentenced to death; Franco commuted the sentence and kept him imprisoned for years. The Carlist leader Manuel Fal Condé had already been sent into exile.", src: "Payne & Palacios, Franco (2014); Preston, Franco (1993)" },
  { l: "walpole", f: "ass", end: "high", text: "When the Excise scheme of 1733 met riots in the streets and a crumbling majority, he withdrew it — and then punished the peers who had organised against him in the Lords: Lord Chesterfield was dismissed as Lord Steward, and Lord Cobham and the Duke of Bolton were deprived of their regiments. The retreat on the policy and the ruthlessness about the people were the same lesson: no one crossed the minister and kept his place.", src: "Plumb, Sir Robert Walpole: The King's Minister; Langford, The Excise Crisis (1975)" },
  { l: "gladstone", f: "ass", end: "high", text: "Queen Victoria's complaint: 'He speaks to Me as if I was a public meeting.' He lectured the sovereign as he lectured everyone; she tried to avoid sending for him in 1880 and did not ask his advice on his successor in 1894.", src: "G. W. E. Russell, Collections and Recollections (1898)" },
  { l: "salisbury", f: "ass", end: "high", text: "In March 1867, as Lord Cranborne, he resigned from Derby's cabinet rather than accept Disraeli's Reform Bill. As he rose to leave, Derby shut his red box and said 'The party is ruined!'; Cranborne walked out without a word, and Lord John Manners found him 'unmoveable'." },
  { l: "baldwin", f: "ass", end: "high", text: "In March 1931, with the Beaverbrook and Rothermere papers backing an anti-Baldwin candidate in the Westminster St George's by-election and the party's principal agent advising in writing that he should go, he came close to resigning. Instead he went to the Queen's Hall on 17 March and turned on the press lords: what they aimed at was 'power without responsibility — the prerogative of the harlot throughout the ages', a line from his cousin Kipling. His candidate, Duff Cooper, won by nearly 6,000 votes.", src: "Middlemas & Barnes, Baldwin (1969); Ball, Baldwin and the Conservative Party (1988)" },
  { l: "macmillan", f: "ass", end: "high", text: "On 13 July 1962, with his government trailing after the Orpington by-election and his plans leaking, he dismissed seven members of his cabinet in one day, starting with his Chancellor and old ally Selwyn Lloyd. The Liberal Jeremy Thorpe adapted the Gospel: greater love hath no man than this, that he lay down his friends for his life.", src: "Contemporary reports; Horne, Macmillan; Thorpe, Supermac" },
  { l: "blair", f: "ass", end: "high", text: "At his first conference as leader, in October 1994, he announced that the party needed a new statement of its aims — without naming Clause IV, its 1918 commitment to common ownership — then toured the constituency parties to win the argument himself. A special conference voted the change through on 29 April 1995.", src: "Seldon, Blair; Blair, A Journey" },
  { l: "grey", f: "ass", end: "high", text: "George Heath, his master and later headmaster at Eton, recalled that Grey 'was as he now is, able in his exercises, impetuous, overbearing'. In May 1832, at sixty-eight, he told William IV that the Reform Bill needed at least fifty new peers, resigned when the King refused, and was back within a week with a written promise." },
  { l: "heath", f: "ass", end: "high", text: "On 20 April 1968 Enoch Powell, his shadow defence spokesman, made the 'rivers of blood' speech on immigration in Birmingham without clearing it with colleagues. Heath dismissed him from the shadow cabinet the next day and never offered him office again, despite the dockers who marched in Powell's support.", src: "Campbell, Edward Heath; Heffer, Like the Roman (1998)" },
  { l: "haroldwilson", f: "ass", end: "low", text: "In June 1969, with the cabinet divided and the unions and much of the parliamentary party against it, he abandoned Barbara Castle's In Place of Strife bill on strikes in return for a 'solemn and binding' undertaking from the TUC — a retreat that his opponents mocked for years.", src: "Castle, The Castle Diaries 1964–70; Pimlott, Harold Wilson" },

  // ---------------------------------------------------------- NOT STRAIGHTFORWARD
  { l: "napoleon", f: "nstr", end: "high", text: "The Bulletins de la Grande Armée were so reliably embellished that 'mentir comme un bulletin' — to lie like a bulletin — entered French as an idiom and stayed there." },
  { l: "bismarck", f: "nstr", end: "high", text: "Handed a telegram from Ems reporting a civil exchange between the King and the French ambassador, he edited it over dinner into something curt enough to read as an insult, released it to the press, and had his war within a fortnight." },
  { l: "cesareborgia", f: "nstr", end: "high", text: "Having used Remirro de Orco to pacify the Romagna by terror, he had him cut in two and left in the square at Cesena with a block and a bloody knife beside him. Machiavelli, who was there, wrote that the people were left 'satisfied and stupefied.'" },
  { l: "fdr", f: "nstr", end: "high", text: "To Morgenthau: 'I never let my left hand know what my right hand is doing.' Visitors routinely left the Oval Office convinced of an agreement he had never actually given, a technique contemporaries described as being 'kissed by the president.'" },
  { l: "clinton", f: "nstr", end: "high", text: "Under oath in 1998: 'It depends upon what the meaning of the word is is.' The parsing was technically defensible and is the single most quoted illustration of the facet in modern politics." },
  { l: "disraeli", f: "nstr", end: "high", text: "On managing the Queen: 'Everyone likes flattery; and when you come to royalty you should lay it on with a trowel.' He said it as a working instruction and it worked for years." },
  { l: "stalin", f: "nstr", end: "high", text: "Purged officials were removed not only from office but from the photographic record — Yezhov, who had run the Terror, was airbrushed out of a picture of himself walking beside Stalin on the Moscow canal." },
  { l: "ieyasu", f: "nstr", end: "high", text: "He justified the destruction of the Toyotomi by objecting to an inscription on a temple bell, reading the characters of his own name split across the text as a deliberate curse. Then, having agreed a truce at Osaka on condition the outer moats be filled, he filled the inner ones too." },
  { l: "kissinger", f: "nstr", end: "high", text: "Ran the opening to Beijing through a feigned stomach illness in Pakistan, and had the FBI wiretap his own National Security Council staff to trace leaks — concealment applied inward as readily as outward." },
  { l: "elizabeth1", f: "nstr", end: "high", text: "Her motto was video et taceo — I see and say nothing. She conducted marriage negotiations in apparent earnest with men she had no intention of marrying, for decades, as a foreign policy." },
  { l: "truman", f: "nstr", end: "low", text: "The sign on his desk read THE BUCK STOPS HERE. He was disliked in Washington partly because he meant it, and because he said what he thought about people who did not." },
  { l: "carter", f: "nstr", end: "low", text: "He campaigned on 'I will never lie to you,' and then as president delivered a televised address telling Americans they were suffering a crisis of confidence — accurate, unwelcome, and politically ruinous." },
  { l: "sanmartin", f: "nstr", end: "low", text: "After a private meeting with Bolívar at Guayaquil in 1822 he concluded the other man was better placed to finish the war, handed over his army, resigned every command, and sailed for Europe. He never publicly explained." },
  { l: "goldameir", f: "nstr", end: "low", text: "Her bluntness was a policy and sometimes a liability: 'We can forgive you for killing our sons, but we will never forgive you for making us kill yours.'" },

  { l: "suharto", f: "nstr", end: "high", text: "The 11 March 1966 order that transferred effective power ran to fewer than 200 words, and its original cannot now be found. When the National Archives were asked to locate it, they had only two copies — one from the army's information centre and one from the State Secretariat — with significant differences between them. Suharto used it the next day to ban the Communist Party." },
  { l: "parkchunghee", f: "nstr", end: "high", text: "After his 1967 victory he promised that, under the two-term limit in the 1963 constitution, he would step down in 1971. The ruling party's assembly majority then pushed through an amendment in 1969 allowing the incumbent a third consecutive term, and he ran in 1971." },
  { l: "mussolini", f: "nstr", end: "high", text: "Italian schoolchildren after the war were taught, as an example of Fascist lies, that he left the light burning in his Palazzo Venezia office all night so that passers-by would think he was always at work for the nation. The historian Guido Bonsaver, working through Mussolini's papers, found that on book censorship at least he was personally and heavily involved — the light was sometimes on for a reason.", src: "Bonsaver, Censorship and Literature in Fascist Italy (2007)" },
  { l: "berlusconi", f: "nstr", end: "high", text: "On 8 May 2001, live on Bruno Vespa's Porta a Porta, he signed a 'Contract with the Italians' with five pledges — lower taxes, less crime, higher minimum pensions, 1.5 million new jobs and a start on 40 per cent of a ten-year public-works plan — and promised not to stand again if he failed to meet at least four of them. The sociologist Luca Ricolfi, auditing it in 2006, found that only the pensions pledge had been fully kept — and the increase applied only to pensioners aged seventy or over — though he put overall fulfilment at about 60 per cent. Berlusconi stood again in 2006 and said the contract had been 80 per cent honoured.", src: "Ricolfi, Tempo scaduto: Il Contratto con gli italiani alla prova dei fatti (2006); Pagella Politica" },
  { l: "franco", f: "nstr", end: "high", text: "At Hendaye on 23 October 1940, with Hitler expecting Spain to join the war, he talked for hours, agreed in principle and committed to nothing: his price — Gibraltar, French Morocco, part of Algeria, grain, fuel and arms — was higher than Hitler could pay without losing Vichy France. Hitler is said to have told Mussolini afterwards that he would rather have three or four teeth pulled than go through it again; the remark is reported second-hand.", src: "Preston, Franco (1993); Payne & Palacios, Franco (2014)" },
  { l: "salazar", f: "nstr", end: "high", text: "The plebiscite of 19 March 1933 approved his corporative constitution with 99.5 per cent of the vote — because abstentions were counted as votes in favour. Of 1,330,258 registered voters, 488,840 stayed away and were recorded as approving.", src: "Official results; Meneses, Salazar" },
  { l: "pinochet", f: "nstr", end: "high", text: "On 23 August 1973 Allende appointed him army commander on the recommendation of Carlos Prats, who believed him a loyal constitutionalist. On Sunday 9 September, with the navy's commander already committed to a coup on the 11th, he was the last of the service chiefs to sign on — and he emerged as its leader. A year later DINA killed Prats in Buenos Aires.", src: "Constable & Valenzuela, A Nation of Enemies (1991); Kornbluh, The Pinochet File (2003)" },
  { l: "pinochet", f: "nstr", end: "high", text: "Asked why he wore dark glasses, he told the journalist María Eugenia Oyarzún that a lie is seen in the eyes — and that he had often lied.", src: "Oyarzún's book of interviews with Pinochet (1999), as quoted in El Mercurio's compilation of his sayings, 10 December 2006" },
  { l: "peron", f: "nstr", end: "high", text: "From exile in Madrid he encouraged the guerrilla 'special formations' as part of the movement while assuring union leaders and the right of his favour. Back in power, on 1 May 1974, he turned on the Montoneros from the balcony of the Casa Rosada, contrasting the unions that had kept the movement alive for twenty years with 'these stupid people who shout' and beardless upstarts; their columns walked out, leaving the Plaza de Mayo half empty.", src: "Text of the speech of 1 May 1974 (Baschetti compilation); James, Resistance and Integration (1988)" },
  { l: "walpole", f: "nstr", end: "high", text: "'All those men have their price', said of the opposition 'patriots' — and the line that survives, 'every man has his price', is a misquotation. Coxe, his first biographer, says the words were perverted by leaving out 'those'; his son Horace Walpole wrote that he never heard his father say all men had their prices. The cynicism is real; the universal maxim is a later improvement.", src: "Coxe, Memoirs of Sir Robert Walpole (1798), vol. 1; Walpoliana (1799)", legend: true },
  { l: "peel", f: "nstr", end: "low", text: "In 1829, having resisted Catholic emancipation for a decade and been elected for Oxford University as its champion, he concluded that civil strife in Ireland was the greater danger and changed sides. Instead of keeping the seat, he resigned it and stood again to let the university's voters judge him; they threw him out, and he came back to the Commons for the rotten borough of Westbury. 'Orange Peel' stuck.", src: "Gash, Mr Secretary Peel; History of Parliament" },
  { l: "palmerston", f: "nstr", end: "high", text: "His own spin-doctor: he wrote articles that friendly newspapers printed as their own leaders, sometimes repeating word for word the despatches he had sent as Foreign Secretary, and leaked or published selected documents to steer opinion. A. J. P. Taylor called him the only prime minister to become an accomplished leader-writer.", src: "Denis Judd, Palmerston (1975), as reviewed in the Victorian Web; Taylor" },
  { l: "gladstone", f: "nstr", end: "low", text: "In 1845 he resigned from Peel's cabinet over the increased grant to the Catholic seminary at Maynooth — and then voted for it, so that no one could say he had changed his published views to keep office. Peel confessed: 'I really have great difficulty sometimes in exactly comprehending what he means.'" },
  { l: "lloydgeorge", f: "nstr", end: "high", text: "In October 1912 he told the Commons that he had not speculated in 'that company' — the British Marconi company, then seeking a government contract and run by the brother of the attorney-general, Rufus Isaacs. He had, through Isaacs, bought shares in its American sister company. When the purchase came out the following year, a select committee split on party lines and he survived.", src: "Wikipedia, Marconi scandal; Grigg, Lloyd George: From Peace to War (1985)" },
  { l: "baldwin", f: "nstr", end: "high", text: "On 12 November 1936 he told the Commons, with what he called 'appalling frankness', that in 1933–34 a pacifist public would not have returned a government that asked for rearmament, and that he could not think of anything more certain to lose an election. Critics, Churchill above all, read it as an admission that he had put the party before the country; he meant it as an account of what democracy would bear.", src: "Hansard, 12 November 1936; Wikipedia, Stanley Baldwin" },
  { l: "attlee", f: "nstr", end: "low", text: "Sacking the junior minister John Parker in 1946, he was asked why. 'Not up to it,' he is said to have replied — and that was the whole interview.", src: "Harold Wilson's memoirs, as retold in later accounts; the wording varies between versions" },
  { l: "macmillan", f: "nstr", end: "high", text: "When his Chancellor Peter Thorneycroft and the Treasury ministers Enoch Powell and Nigel Birch resigned over spending in January 1958, he left on schedule for a six-week Commonwealth tour, telling reporters at London Airport on 7 January that the government's affairs were in good order and the resignations were 'a little local difficulty'.", src: "Statement at London Airport, 7 January 1958; Horne, Macmillan" },
  { l: "blair", f: "nstr", end: "high", text: "In May 1994, after John Smith's death, he and Gordon Brown met at the Granita restaurant in Islington, and Brown agreed not to stand for the leadership. What Blair offered in return — control of economic and social policy, and some understanding about handing over later — was never written down, and the two men's camps remembered it differently for thirteen years.", src: "Seldon, Blair; accounts of both camps" },
  { l: "liverpool", f: "nstr", end: "low", text: "His Victorian biographer C. D. Yonge praised his 'undeviating freedom from jobbery of every kind' and noted that he left office a poorer man than he entered it; Asa Briggs added that he never dismissed a minister and was 'never ungrateful or disloyal'. Disraeli's jibe was not that he was devious, but that he was 'peremptory in little questions' and left the great ones open.", src: "Yonge, Life of Lord Liverpool (1868); Briggs (ed.), The Prime Ministers (1974); Disraeli, Coningsby (1844)" },
  { l: "haroldwilson", f: "nstr", end: "high", text: "In public he was the man with the pipe and the Gannex raincoat, who kept his Yorkshire accent and liked HP Sauce; the British Pipesmokers' Council named him Pipe Smoker of the Year in 1965. In private he preferred cigars.", src: "Widely reported; Pimlott, Harold Wilson" },
  { l: "heath", f: "nstr", end: "low", text: "Beaten by Margaret Thatcher on the first ballot in February 1975, he refused a place in her shadow cabinet and spent the next quarter-century making his disagreement plain — 'the incredible sulk', in the press's phrase. Told of her resignation in November 1990 he is reported to have said 'Rejoice, rejoice'; he later corrected the story, saying he had said it three times.", src: "Campbell, Edward Heath (1993), p. 787; Ziegler, Edward Heath" },

  // ---------------------------------------------------------- POSITIVE EMOTIONS
  { l: "troosevelt", f: "pos", end: "high", text: "His daughter Alice: 'Father always wanted to be the corpse at every funeral, the bride at every wedding, and the baby at every christening.'" },
  { l: "reagan", f: "pos", end: "high", text: "Shot in the chest in 1981, he told Nancy 'Honey, I forgot to duck,' and greeted the surgical team with 'I hope you're all Republicans.' The lead surgeon, a Democrat, answered that today they were all Republicans." },
  { l: "clinton", f: "pos", end: "high", text: "The standing description of his retail talent is that he made whoever he was speaking to feel like the only person in the room — reported by supporters and by people who had gone in intending to dislike him." },
  { l: "harding", f: "pos", end: "high", text: "He was genuinely and universally liked, which was the entire basis of his nomination: a smoke-filled room chose him because nobody could think of a reason to object to him." },
  { l: "lula", f: "pos", end: "high", text: "A metalworker who lost a finger to a press, he retained a physical warmth in crowds that survived a corruption conviction, 580 days in prison and a return to the presidency at seventy-seven." },
  { l: "jcaesar", f: "pos", end: "high", text: "The sources describe a man who enjoyed being Caesar — witty, lavishly generous, an excellent host, and genuinely loved by legions who followed him across a Rubicon they knew was illegal." },
  { l: "coolidge", f: "pos", end: "low", text: "Alice Roosevelt Longworth said he looked as though he had been weaned on a pickle. Told that Coolidge had died, Dorothy Parker is supposed to have asked, 'How could they tell?'", legend: true },
  { l: "nixon", f: "pos", end: "low", text: "He bowled alone in the White House lanes, found small talk an ordeal, and once had an aide draft a memo on how he might appear more spontaneous. The discomfort in company is visible in almost every unguarded photograph." },
  { l: "richelieu", f: "pos", end: "low", text: "Chronically ill, feared rather than liked, and by his own account at the end without a single friend — asked on his deathbed whether he forgave his enemies, he answered that he had none save those of the state." },
  { l: "frederick2p", f: "pos", end: "low", text: "He grew steadily more misanthropic and ended his life keeping the company of his Italian greyhounds in preference to people, leaving instructions to be buried beside them rather than with his family." },
  { l: "hoover", f: "pos", end: "low", text: "He and his wife dressed formally for dinner every evening in the White House, including when dining alone, and he was unable to project warmth or hope at precisely the moment when both were the job." },
  { l: "pericles", f: "pos", end: "low", text: "Plutarch notes that in all his years of leading Athens he was never seen to laugh, never attended a friend's entertainment, and walked only one route — from his house to the agora." },
  { l: "berlusconi", f: "pos", end: "high", text: "As a student he played the double bass in a band with his schoolfriend Fedele Confalonieri and sang on cruise ships; decades later, as prime minister, he wrote the lyrics for albums of Neapolitan-style love songs recorded by the singer Mariano Apicella.", src: "Contemporary press reports" },
  { l: "salazar", f: "pos", end: "low", text: "Never married, he lived at the São Bento residence with the housekeeper who had followed him from Coimbra and two girls from her family whom he took in, rarely went out, disliked crowds and avoided public ceremony. He seldom left Portugal, and in thirteen years of colonial war never visited Africa; his housekeeper's reports from the Lisbon markets were among his windows on ordinary life.", src: "Meneses, Salazar; Joaquim Vieira, A Governanta" },
  { l: "walpole", f: "pos", end: "high", text: "In the parliamentary recesses he filled Houghton with ministers, members and Norfolk neighbours for the 'Houghton congress': hunting by day, enormous dinners and drinking by night, politics in between. Chesterfield, no friend, called him 'inelegant in his manners, loose in his morals' and gifted with 'a coarse wit'.", src: "Plumb, Sir Robert Walpole; Chesterfield's character of Walpole" },
  { l: "pittyounger", f: "pos", end: "low", text: "To the House and to strangers he was icy: contemporaries described a cold, stiff, unbending figure who walked up the floor of the Commons looking neither to right nor left. The same man was, by Wilberforce's account, the liveliest of companions among the friends of Goostree's club and at Holwood — playful, given to practical jokes, and close to almost nobody else.", src: "Wraxall, Historical Memoirs; Wilberforce's recollections, in Hague, William Pitt the Younger" },
  { l: "peel", f: "pos", end: "low", text: "His smile, a famous jibe ran, was like the silver plate on a coffin. The line is variously given to Daniel O'Connell and to the Irish orator John Philpot Curran, with no secure source for either — but the reputation behind it was universal. Even admirers found him shy, stiff and awkward with strangers, and his backbenchers felt he never thanked them.", src: "Attributed; collected on Wikiquote without a source", legend: true },
  { l: "palmerston", f: "pos", end: "high", text: "A Frenchman, meaning to compliment him, said: 'If I were not a Frenchman, I should wish to be an Englishman.' Palmerston: 'If I were not an Englishman, I should wish to be an Englishman.'", src: "As retold by Gladstone in 1889" },
  { l: "salisbury", f: "pos", end: "low", text: "When his brother complained in 1867 of being accosted by constituents in a hotel: 'A hotel infested by influential constituents is worse than one infested by bugs. It's a pity you can't carry around a powder insecticide to get rid of vermin of that kind.'" },
  { l: "attlee", f: "pos", end: "low", text: "Famously laconic with the press — at one interview he got through twenty-eight questions in five minutes — and at the 1953 party conference at Margate he ate his meals alone. He described himself as diffident and as a poor subject for any publicity expert.", src: "Bew, Citizen Clem, as summarised in the New York Review of Books (2018)" },
  { l: "blair", f: "pos", end: "high", text: "At his first cabinet meeting in May 1997 he asked ministers to call him Tony rather than 'Prime Minister' — informality that his admirers saw as a new style and his critics as the first sign of a government run from the sofa.", src: "Widely reported at the time; Seldon, Blair" },
  { l: "liverpool", f: "pos", end: "low", text: "Harriet Arbuthnot, wife of his own Treasury secretary, wrote in 1821 that he had 'a disagreeable, cold manner and a most querulous, irritable temper'; a colleague said he had fewer personal friends than any minister who ever lived. Disraeli's verdict in Coningsby was 'the Arch-Mediocrity who presided, rather than ruled, over this Cabinet of Mediocrities'.", src: "Mrs Arbuthnot's Journal; Hay, Lord Liverpool; Disraeli, Coningsby (1844)" },
  { l: "heath", f: "pos", end: "low", text: "Explaining why Conservative MPs deserted him in 1975, the political journalist Alan Watkins listed 'his brusqueness, his gaucherie, his lack of small or indeed any talk, his sheer bad manners'.", src: "Alan Watkins, A Conservative Coup (1991); Campbell, Edward Heath" },

  // ---------------------------------------------------------- TENDER-MINDEDNESS
  { l: "lincoln", f: "ten", end: "high", text: "He pardoned deserters and sleeping sentries at a rate that infuriated his generals, calling them his 'leg cases' — men whose legs had run away with them — and said he could not bring himself to have a boy shot for being frightened." },
  { l: "saladin", f: "ten", end: "high", text: "Learning that Richard the Lionheart was ill with fever at Jaffa, he sent him fruit chilled with snow from Mount Hermon and his own physician — to an enemy commander, in the middle of a siege." },
  { l: "ashoka", f: "ten", end: "high", text: "After the conquest of Kalinga he had his own remorse carved into rock across the empire: that the slaughter weighed on him, and that he now considered conquest by dhamma the only conquest worth having. No other ancient ruler published his own regret." },
  { l: "mandela", f: "ten", end: "high", text: "He learned Afrikaans in prison to understand his jailers, invited one of his former warders to his inauguration, and later took the prosecutor who had sought his death sentence out to lunch." },
  { l: "marcusaurelius", f: "ten", end: "high", text: "Rather than raise war taxes during the Marcomannic wars he auctioned imperial treasures — palace furniture, his wife's silk and gold — in the Forum of Trajan for two months, and afterwards let buyers return the goods for refunds if they wished." },
  { l: "ghwbush", f: "ten", end: "high", text: "He wrote handwritten notes compulsively for decades, and left one in the Oval Office desk for the man who had just beaten him: 'Your success now is our country's success. I am rooting hard for you.'" },
  { l: "carter", f: "ten", end: "high", text: "His post-presidency has run four decades: Habitat for Humanity, election monitoring, and a Guinea worm eradication campaign that has taken global cases from an estimated 3.5 million a year to double digits." },
  { l: "ardern", f: "ten", end: "high", text: "Within days of the Christchurch mosque attacks she appeared in a headscarf, embraced the bereaved, refused to speak the killer's name, and had the gun laws changed inside twenty-six days." },
  { l: "napoleon", f: "ten", end: "low", text: "Metternich reports him saying that a man like him does not concern himself with the lives of a million men. The source is hostile and the remark has been doubted; nothing else in the record makes it implausible.", src: "Metternich, Memoirs" },
  { l: "peter1", f: "ten", end: "low", text: "He had his son and heir Alexei interrogated under torture for suspected disloyalty and was present for it. Alexei died in the Peter and Paul Fortress two days after the second session." },
  { l: "suleiman", f: "ten", end: "low", text: "Persuaded that his eldest son Mustafa was plotting, he summoned him to the imperial camp and had him strangled by mutes in the next tent while he listened." },
  { l: "chiang", f: "ten", end: "low", text: "In June 1938, to slow the Japanese advance, he ordered the Yellow River dikes breached at Huayuankou without warning the population. The flood killed several hundred thousand of his own citizens and displaced millions." },
  { l: "jcaesar", f: "ten", end: "low", text: "At Uxellodunum he spared the lives of the surrendered garrison and had the hands of every man who had borne arms cut off, so that the punishment would be visible wherever they went.", src: "Caesar, Gallic War VIII" },
  { l: "timur", f: "ten", end: "low", text: "At Isfahan, after a tax revolt, his army was required to deliver a quota of severed heads; the towers built from them were counted by contemporaries in the tens of thousands, and were addressed to the next city on the road." },
  { l: "stalin", f: "ten", end: "low", text: "'One death is a tragedy, a million is a statistic' is universally attributed to him and appears in no document he produced. It persists because the documented record — the signed execution lists, the quotas by region — makes it entirely credible.", legend: true },
  { l: "qinshihuang", f: "ten", end: "low", text: "The tradition holds that he had 460 scholars buried alive for possessing prohibited texts. Han historians had every reason to exaggerate, and the burning of the books is beyond dispute.", legend: true },
  { l: "suharto", f: "ten", end: "low", text: "Acknowledged the 1983–85 'mysterious shootings' only in his autobiography, where he wrote that 'we had to resort to force' and that those who resisted 'had to be shot'. Estimates of the dead range from about 300 to 10,000; many victims were tattooed men, whose bodies were left in public places.", src: "Soeharto: Pikiran, Ucapan dan Tindakan Saya (with G. Dwipayana and Ramadhan K.H.)" },
  { l: "parkchunghee", f: "ten", end: "low", text: "On 8 August 1973 KCIA agents kidnapped the opposition leader Kim Dae-jung from a Tokyo hotel and took him by boat toward Korea. By Kim's own account his kidnappers meant to drown him in the Korea Strait, and gave up when Japan's Maritime Self-Defense Force began a pursuit and fired an illuminating shell; he was found alive at his Seoul home five days later. In 2007 South Korea's intelligence service admitted the KCIA had done it, with at least tacit backing from Park.", src: "NIS inquiry, October 2007" },
  { l: "mussolini", f: "ten", end: "low", text: "In the Ethiopian war of 1935–36 he personally authorised the use of poison gas, including mustard gas sprayed from aircraft, against Ethiopian soldiers and the villages behind them, in breach of the 1925 Geneva Protocol Italy had ratified; the regime denied it, and Italy officially admitted it only in 1996.", src: "Angelo Del Boca; Mack Smith, Mussolini" },
  { l: "franco", f: "ten", end: "low", text: "In September 1975, two months before his death and already ill with Parkinson's disease, he and his cabinet confirmed five of eleven death sentences passed on members of ETA and the FRAP. Appeals for clemency came from Pope Paul VI and governments across Europe; the five were shot on 27 September, several countries recalled their ambassadors, and the Spanish embassy in Lisbon was sacked. On 1 October he appeared before a crowd in the Plaza de Oriente and blamed the protests on a leftist-Masonic conspiracy.", src: "Preston, Franco (1993); contemporary press" },
  { l: "salazar", f: "ten", end: "low", text: "In 1936 his government opened the Tarrafal camp on the Cape Verdean island of Santiago for political prisoners. Its inmates called it the 'camp of slow death': of the 340 Portuguese prisoners sent there before it closed in 1954, 34 died, many of disease in a camp with little medical care. It reopened in 1961 for prisoners from the African independence movements.", src: "Museu do Tarrafal; Meneses, Salazar" },
  { l: "pinochet", f: "ten", end: "low", text: "During the coup a radio amateur recorded the communications between the commanders. On the tapes Pinochet rejects any negotiated surrender and, discussing the offer to fly Allende out of the country, adds that the plane will fall once it is in the air; the admiral on the other end laughs. Allende killed himself in La Moneda that afternoon. The recordings were later published by the journalist Patricia Verdugo in Interferencia secreta.", src: "Verdugo, Interferencia secreta (book and recording); transcripts in the Chilean press" },
  { l: "peron", f: "ten", end: "low", text: "On 31 August 1955, two months after navy aircraft had bombed the Plaza de Mayo, he answered the opposition from the balcony with a threat that became notorious: whoever tried to subvert the constitutional order could be killed by any Argentine, and for every one of ours who falls, five of theirs will fall. Two weeks later he was overthrown.", src: "Text of the speech in La Prensa, 1 September 1955 (educ.ar archive); Page, Perón (1983)" },
  { l: "pittyounger", f: "ten", end: "high", text: "On 2 April 1792 he spoke through the night for the abolition of the slave trade, in what many who heard it, Fox and Windham among them, ranked with the finest speeches they had heard. As he reached his peroration, imagining Africa one day joining the civilised world, the dawn came up through the windows of the House, and he quoted Virgil on the rising sun. The Commons voted for gradual abolition; the Lords stalled it, and Pitt never made it a government measure.", src: "Hague, William Pitt the Younger; Parliamentary History, 2 April 1792" },
  { l: "peel", f: "ten", end: "high", text: "In November 1845, with the potato blight spreading in Ireland, he authorised the secret purchase of about £100,000 of Indian corn from the United States through Barings, to be released to hold down food prices — on his own authority, while the cabinet was still divided over the Corn Laws. His resignation speech of 29 June 1846 hoped to be remembered in the homes of working men who would eat 'abundant and untaxed food, the sweeter because it is no longer leavened by a sense of injustice'.", src: "Gash, Sir Robert Peel; Hansard, 29 June 1846" },
  { l: "palmerston", f: "ten", end: "high", text: "In 1818 a half-pay lieutenant with a pension grievance shot him on the War Office stairs; the bullet grazed his back. When he learned the man was mentally ill, Palmerston paid for his defence at the trial, and the man was sent to Bethlem hospital." },
  { l: "baldwin", f: "ten", end: "high", text: "In June 1919, as Financial Secretary to the Treasury, he wrote to The Times under the initials 'FST' urging the rich to impose on themselves a voluntary levy to pay down the war debt — and anonymously gave about £120,000 of War Loan, a fifth of his fortune, to be cancelled. Few followed; the secret held for years.", src: "The Times, 24 June 1919; Middlemas & Barnes, Baldwin (1969)" },
  { l: "attlee", f: "ten", end: "high", text: "Went in 1905 to help at Haileybury House, his old school's boys' club in Stepney, and stayed on for years as its manager. The poverty of the East End turned a Conservative-minded young barrister into a socialist, and later into Stepney's mayor and the MP for Limehouse.", src: "Attlee, As It Happened; Harris, Attlee" },
  { l: "grey", f: "ten", end: "high", text: "On 23 February 1807, as Foreign Secretary in the dying Ministry of All the Talents, he moved the bill abolishing the British slave trade, which the Commons carried by 283 votes to 16. Twenty-six years later his own government passed the act abolishing slavery itself throughout the Empire — though with £20 million in compensation for the owners and years of 'apprenticeship' for the freed." },

];
